From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 12:41:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11063/11171] Correct the third instance: induction_attempts
 was present, nested

My 2026-09-04 pattern entry lists three cases of the live eval failing to record
a field a consumer needed. The third is wrong at the level I stated it.

I tested `'induction_attempts' in row`, got False, and reported the field absent.
It is present inside policy_diagnostics with four rounds, one per emitted engine,
and it landed before my check. I tested a top-level key and reported the result as
a property of the artifact.

That is the trap written in my own memory file under "field names lie -- read the
content", committed by me, on the same day.

The real gap was the round-level fields engine_emitted_at, prompt_sha256 and
transition_source_path, plus a transition-source file format both exp6968 and
exp6981 were written against that nothing produced. exp6968 blocked for a genuine
missing-field reason and I named the wrong field.

The pattern itself survives all three instances -- a consumer written against a
field the producer does not emit, found only when the consumer comes back empty --
and the prevention check built for it proves the point rather than depending on my
error: run before the fix, it flagged exactly those three round-level fields.

Records the lesson the entry should have carried: a nested field is invisible to a
top-level membership test and its False reads exactly like absence, so a
missing-field claim needs the container's keys dumped or the consumer's own
accessor used.

